# ~pgaupload/.ssh/authorized_keys on the download server (ftp.pgadmin.org)
#
# The same two keys as on the staging server, with the same options and for
# the same reasons; read the annotations in authorized_keys.staging first,
# since they are not repeated here. What differs is the blast radius, because
# this host serves the packages that end users install, and the yum and apt
# signing keys live here.
#
# Placeholders in angle brackets must be substituted at install time:
#
#   <...KEY...>   the public key, one line, no line breaks
#
# Note that these are the same two key pairs as on the staging server, not
# four. One publishing key and one upload key, each trusted by both hosts, each
# confined
# differently on each host by the options below. Two more key pairs would mean
# two more things to rotate and no reduction in what a compromised runner can
# reach, since a compromised runner has both of them anyway.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key 1: publishing. Verbs only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a holder of this key can do here: create a v<VERSION> directory, which
# it cannot do twice, or a snapshot directory, which is deliberately
# repeatable; pull the matching staging content from the staging server;
# rebuild and sign the apt and yum metadata for an allowlisted distribution;
# and run the four existing publication scripts with arguments
# that have been validated or allowlisted first.
#
# What it cannot do, and this is the point of the exercise: choose something
# to delete, name a file, name a path, name a host, name a signing key, choose
# which binary runs, or supply a single character that reaches a shell.
#
# Nor can it change anything already published, which is a separate claim and
# worth stating separately, because the verbs that add content are the ones
# that would otherwise overwrite it. release-fetch refuses to run at all if
# v<VERSION> holds anything beyond the markers release-create left, and
# refuses each file whose destination exists, so a promotion is single-shot
# end to end rather than only at its first verb; packages-fetch pulls with
# --ignore-existing, so a package already live keeps the bytes its signed
# metadata describes and the mirrors already hold. A release that needs
# correcting gets a new version.
restrict,command="/usr/local/sbin/pga-publish" ssh-ed25519 <...PUBLISH-KEY...> pga-publish@runner

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key 2: upload. Bytes only, and only into the snapshots tree.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Snapshots are built straight onto this server rather than staged elsewhere
# and promoted, so unlike the release flow they do need an upload path here.
#
# The confinement root is the snapshots tree itself, which is the right place
# on two counts. It is where snapshots belong, and it is the one part of the
# download tree that sync-ftp-to-s3.py excludes, so nothing written through
# this key can reach the archive bucket or the PostgreSQL mirrors. A key
# confined anywhere else under /var/ftp would have both of those as a side
# effect of a single bad upload.
#
# Retention is not this key's business either, and it cannot be made to be.
# -no-del means this key cannot remove a snapshot even if asked to, and
# snapshot-purge keeps the newest SNAPSHOT_KEEP dates rather than the newest
# that many directories, so an upload cannot evict a real snapshot by arriving
# under today's date with a high suffix. rrsync confines where an upload lands
# but has nothing to say about what it is called, which is precisely why
# retention must not turn on names the client chose.
#
# SITE: check `command -v rrsync`; the path moved between rsync packages.
restrict,command="/usr/bin/rrsync -wo -no-del -munge /var/ftp/pgadmin4/snapshots" ssh-ed25519 <...UPLOAD-KEY...> pga-upload@runner
